-- Schema version 2: the shape shipped before `mounts` grew a `base_oid`
-- column, i.e. before every read-write mount had its OWN pinned base.
--
-- Captured with the v0.2.1 binary (CURRENT_SCHEMA_VERSION = 2), not written by
-- hand, and then frozen: only the millisecond timestamps were rewritten to
-- fixed values so the fixture is deterministic. Do not regenerate from current
-- code. See README.md.
--
-- What it deliberately contains, because these are the states MIGRATE_2_TO_3
-- has to decide about:
--
--   * session A holds FOUR mounts: `/` read-write on `ref:base`, `/other`
--     read-write on `ref:other` (a second writable base -- the defect), `/dep`
--     read-only on `ref:other`, and `/snap` read-write on a raw `oid:` (a write
--     path with no publish path). It has staged overlay under `/`, `/other` and
--     `/snap`, and observations recorded through `/other` and `/dep`. The
--     `/other` observation was recorded against `ref:base`'s tree, because that
--     is what v2 served through a read-write mount.
--   * session B is the ordinary shape: the default `/` mount on its own
--     `heads/agents/...` live ref, staged overlay, and a directory observation.
--   * `base` and `other` have diverged, `tags/v0` is a sealed snapshot, two
--     abandoned sessions left their `heads/agents/...` refs behind, and
--     `cap_root.hmac_key` is already scrubbed.
--
-- `base` holds a7e174d5..., which is also session A's and session B's
-- `pinned_oid`; `other` holds 87fa249d.... The migration must leave session B's
-- `/` pinned at a7e174d5... and must move session A's `/other` to 87fa249d...,
-- and it must demote `/snap` to `ro`.

